00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:10.250
CHEN GOLDBERG: Hi.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:11.640
Good afternoon, everybody.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.800
I'm Chen Goldberg.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:13.270
APARNA SINHA: And
I'm Aparna Sinha.

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:15.840
CHEN GOLDBERG: And thank
you for joining us today.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.690
It was super exciting
for us to see

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.180
Urs presenting the
Cloud Services Platform

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.000
today in his keynote.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.650
We started working
on this vision a bit

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:28.010
over four years ago, where we
took our years of experience

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.490
in developing applications
and managing them reliably,

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:33.080
and brought them
through the industry

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.620
through Kubernetes
and Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.940
We invited community, users,
partners, and customers

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.790
to work with us, ensuring
Kubernetes solves

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:48.110
real problems for all users.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.440
The result is that
Kubernetes today

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.870
is the industry standard
for managing containers

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:55.490
applications.

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.600
It is used by enterprises of
all sizes in their journey

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.750
to modernisation, increasing
teams' productivity

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.660
and leapfrogging competition.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.180
Last year, we open
sourced Istio,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.570
decoupling service development
from service management,

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.730
and it already got significant
traction from the industry,

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.510
including first
users in production.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.840
Today, we are bringing those
technologies and a few new ones

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.380
together, building an
integrated stack, giving you

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.549
application modernization
wherever you are, in the cloud

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:27.215
and on premises.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.200
Our new solution is built
with those four principles

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.070
in mind, consistent experience,
centralized control,

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:40.200
agility with reliability
and flexibility.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.550
We'll be using them to
introduce our products.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.510
It will help you understand why
we have built Cloud Services

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:50.700
Platform the way we have,
and what to expect next.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.540
If they sound
familiar to you, it

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.920
is because they are already
rooted in Kubernetes.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.630
They are also demonstrated
in many other Google Cloud

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:01.510
Platform products
and solutions today.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.679
You will hear more of them
throughout the next three days.

00:02:04.679 --> 00:02:06.220
APARNA SINHA: And
we want you to know

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.530
that these principles
are not just principles

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.660
for technical design.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.380
They are principles that
enable a customer journey that

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:19.210
starts with you, where you are,
and allows you to transform IT

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.800
at your own pace.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.725
We all know that
enterprise IT is complex.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:31.010
Every customer that I work
with has a mix of custom

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.160
developed software as well
as off the shelf software,

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.670
and a variety of on-prem
hardware from many vendors.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.670
And across that, they
have to ensure security,

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.790
regulatory compliance, and
all of their policies work

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.220
seamlessly.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:51.300
This complexity can
slow businesses down.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.920
In the middle of
this environment,

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.160
cloud offers an
opportunity for speed

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.590
and competitive advantage.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.560
But without the
right software, cloud

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.380
can sometimes add another
layer of heterogeneity

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.840
to an already
complex environment.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.300
Cloud Services
Platform is targeting

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.060
the 80% of enterprises
that are looking

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.690
to enable hybrid cloud today.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:21.010
They need a way to homogenize
public cloud infrastructure

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:24.220
with what they have on-prem,
connecting and managing it

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:26.530
together.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:30.310
I see users marrying the
modern with the existing,

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:34.390
so that they can really
enable use cases like these.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.520
They want to have CI/CD--

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.760
Continuous Integration,
Continuous Deployment-- across

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.830
public and private.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:44.690
And they want to
be able to manage

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:49.130
compute at the edge, where
they can have low latency,

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:52.650
or for regulatory reasons,
or just for business reasons,

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:56.520
and yet have centralized
intelligence in the cloud.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.760
And then lastly, they
want to be able to consume

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.490
the best-of-breed services,
regardless of where they're

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.460
running, whether in their
on-prem data center,

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.600
in GCP, or in any cloud.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.540
Today, to accomplish
these use cases

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:16.610
requires a lot of fragile,
do-it-yourself integration.

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:21.290
Or, it can result
in a compromise

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:25.220
between consistency and
choice, between agility

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.340
and reliability.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.910
Cloud Service Platform is a
different kind of hybrid cloud

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:35.900
software that overcomes
some of these trade-offs,

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.910
so you don't have to
make those compromises.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:41.420
At its core, Cloud
Services Platform

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:44.480
is powered by Kubernetes
and Istio, which

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.220
are open source, enabling that
consistency across environment.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:54.950
But on top of that, this
stack is custom-configured,

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:58.730
enterprise-hardened, tested
and managed by Google,

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:02.500
so you have that
centralized control.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.340
It is also deeply
integrated with GCP services

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:10.260
to deliver the benefits
of Google Cloud On-Prem.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.460
And then, this integration
enables a wide variety

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.600
of solutions, such as
CI/CD, across clouds--

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.910
also machine learning
and serverless

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.580
and a variety of others.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.670
Today, Chen and I are
going to demo for you

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.970
exactly how we break some of
the traditional trade-offs

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:33.064
with these design principles.

00:05:33.064 --> 00:05:34.480
CHEN GOLDBERG: The
first principle

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.280
of Cloud Services Platform
is consistent experience.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.170
We have seen with Kubernetes
how much consistency matters.

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:45.250
The idea of write
once, run anywhere,

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.550
pulled users and partners
into its community.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:52.680
No lock-in to a specific
platform really excites users.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:56.320
It simplifies things for
developers and operation teams

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:59.260
when they know there is a
certain set of primitives

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:00.100
they can rely on.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:06.670
Cloud Services Platform
takes consistency further.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:08.940
Portability isn't enough.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.540
Users are looking for
consistent user experience,

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:17.130
use the same tools everywhere,
where their workloads run.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:18.510
Their operations
teams would like

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:22.440
to have the same way to
manage the cluster, on-premise

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.000
and in the cloud.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.060
Developers would like to
develop, build, and deploy

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.580
the application the same
way, regardless of where

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:31.320
the workloads will run.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.020
The SLA team would like to
have the same tools in order

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:39.550
to troubleshoot an issue,
regardless of an environment.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:43.210
Our customers tell us they
want a consistent experience

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:46.390
of Kubernetes Engine, GKE.

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:49.880
GKE has been GA for
almost three years now,

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.690
and users like Kubernetes
Engine, automation,

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:55.400
upstream currency,
and reliability.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.370
It is Google-configured,
managed,

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:59.200
and supported Kubernetes.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.030
They've been asking
us, how can they

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:06.190
get the same capabilities in
their on-prem data centers?

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:08.860
And we are giving our
customers exactly that.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.760
Introducing GKE On-Prem.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.820
It is Google-configured
Kubernetes,

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:16.940
with automated provisioning,
managed upgrades, and security

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:19.940
in your on-prem environment.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:23.510
When talking with users, this
is when their eyes light up.

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:27.170
The idea that they don't have to
train their teams once or twice

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:29.930
to learn different tool
really excites them.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.570
This is where they are
spending most of their time.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:35.480
Operation teams having
a single playbook

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:37.490
to manage all of
their environments

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:40.250
is really a
game-changer for them.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:43.430
Less time spent on
training and integration

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:46.180
leaves more time to innovation,
and increases the team

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.280
productivity.

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:50.790
What's even more awesome
about GKE On-Prem,

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:54.300
that it connects seamlessly
to Google Cloud Platform,

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.880
and helps you manage a
multi-cluster environment

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:58.710
wherever your cluster is.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.130
And all of them will look
and behave exactly the same.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.260
Let's move to the demo and
see consistency in action.

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:14.060
OK, so here, what
we have already

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:16.090
is that earlier this
morning, my team

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:19.130
provisioned a GKE On-Prem
cluster on this pizza box

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:20.806
server running vSphere.

00:08:20.806 --> 00:08:22.670
Voila!

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:25.220
This is all live demo.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:28.940
And now I will show you how
to integrate this cluster

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:33.440
into your GKE environment and
get that consistent experience.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:40.370
From the GKE UI, I can go and
register an external cluster.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:45.075
I will call it the
innovative name West.

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:52.305
And then I will download
this GKE Connect manifest.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:57.180
Let's look inside it.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:01.190
What it will help us is to
create that connection to GCP.

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:05.330
Oh, sorry.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.100
So what it has inside,
it has a namespace.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.600
And in addition to that, there
is a service account and role

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:19.630
bindings, [INAUDIBLE] in the
UI in order to access and view

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:22.550
the workloads in the cluster.

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:26.770
We also have here a deployment
of the GKE Connect agent, which

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:29.680
is what we use in order to
create a secure connection

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.410
from this cluster into GCP.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:34.160
Let's apply it.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:40.810
You can also see
here in this UI,

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:44.590
that GKE is waiting for GKE
Connect connection with Google.

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:48.360
And once it's done, we will see
this change into a check mark.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:49.521
Yay!

00:09:49.521 --> 00:09:51.020
And we'll continue
to the next step.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:56.995
Here, I have to provide
some more details.

00:09:56.995 --> 00:09:58.370
Also, I want to
make sure that we

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:01.050
are talking with the right
cluster from GCP's side.

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:08.420
So I will use a
certificate, provide the IP,

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:12.160
and continue to authenticate.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:13.930
For the sake of the
demo, I will just

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:15.550
skip authentication for now.

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:19.990
But I can also use a user
token, or leverage an identity

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:24.770
provider, in order to
do basic authentication.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:25.730
And we are finished.

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:28.400
What we can see right
now, in the environment,

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:31.790
in my GKE environment, I
can see all my clusters,

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:33.870
including this on-prem cluster.

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:35.780
And it is already registered.

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:37.490
But you can also
see that I don't

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:40.440
have any details about it.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.280
Everybody needs to enter
their own user credentials,

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:46.340
and this is why there
is this lock icon here.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:48.490
This is good hygiene
practice in general,

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:51.230
and it helps us to maintain the
integrity of the environment

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:54.840
and make sure that we have the
right audit logging and RBAC

00:10:54.840 --> 00:10:55.340
policies.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.434
I will copy it from
the environment.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:17.200
And now I will actually get
access into the cluster.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:19.470
And you can see how I
can see the cluster size,

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:21.630
the number of CPU, and more.

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.400
From this point on,
everything actually

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:25.875
behaves exactly the
same as the GKE clusters

00:11:25.875 --> 00:11:30.020
you are used to
managing on GKE UI.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:30.520
Yes.

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:31.630
First live demo.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:33.430
Yay.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:35.180
We see the master
version, for example,

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:36.180
which is very important.

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:39.250
Again, another attribute
that we keep consistent

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:40.540
with your GKE environment.

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:43.120
We heard from our customers
this is really important

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:45.130
for them, for the
integration aspect

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:47.500
they have to GKE
and to Kubernetes.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.440
We can see the nodes
that are there.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:54.850
And like every other GKE
cluster, I can go into the node

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:56.530
and see really
useful information,

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:59.020
such as the pods
running, and resources

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:00.880
available on that node.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:03.130
So this is just looking
on a single cluster.

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:07.270
But then, GKE actually creates
this multi-cluster single pane

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:09.890
of glass, where I
can see, for example,

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:12.190
all the workloads running
in my environment,

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:15.830
regardless of which cluster
they are running on.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:20.100
And this is true also
for services as well.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:22.610
So this is really nice,
getting that visibility

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:23.990
into my environment.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:26.860
But what's even more
awesome-- and again,

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:28.490
first time doing it onstage--

00:12:28.490 --> 00:12:32.090
I will also deploy, from
GCP, for the first time,

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:35.670
into a cluster which is
actually not a GCP cluster.

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:37.173
And it can run anywhere.

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:43.460
We'll go into Deploy.

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:46.550
And we really wanted to make
sure it's simple as possible.

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:50.790
And all I need to do is to
choose the right cluster.

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:54.000
I will click Deploy.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.650
And what do you think?

00:12:57.650 --> 00:12:59.340
Dun, dun, dun.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:00.270
Voila!

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:01.395
Yay!

00:13:01.395 --> 00:13:05.120
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:06.810
And while it really
looks simple,

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:09.040
it's actually not simple to do.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:11.640
And for us, we heard
from our customers

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:14.430
that they really appreciate
the management capabilities

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:16.020
they have on GKE.

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:18.454
And we want to provide
that wherever customers

00:13:18.454 --> 00:13:19.620
are running their workloads.

00:13:24.929 --> 00:13:25.720
Back to the slides.

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:38.790
This is a quote
that I really like,

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:40.870
since it captures
really what excites

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:44.140
me the most about the
technology we are building.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:45.970
You know, I'm an
infrastructure person.

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:49.330
But what excites me is that
we are allowing developers

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:54.580
to really free their time
and focus on innovation.

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:55.755
APARNA SINHA: Awesome.

00:13:55.755 --> 00:13:57.720
Consistency is
the building block

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.960
for the next principle of
Cloud Services Platform, which

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:03.720
is centralized control.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.130
We all know that
control is important.

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:08.490
But what is different about
Cloud Services Platform

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:09.910
is two things.

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:13.500
Number one, it provides control
not just across clusters,

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:16.770
but across environments,
so across GCP

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:18.360
and other environments.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:20.610
Number two, it
works out of the box

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:22.384
with your existing
authentication

00:14:22.384 --> 00:14:23.175
and other services.

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:30.910
So it doesn't create a trade-off
between control and agility.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:35.290
As a complement to Chen's
announcement about GKE On-Prem,

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:38.210
we're also really excited
to announce GKE Policy

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:42.540
Manager, which is in alpha now.

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:46.770
This is a piece of software that
allows admins to define tenant

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:49.560
namespaces, as well
as policies that can

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:53.010
be applied across environments.

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:55.440
And these are defined
in a single source

00:14:55.440 --> 00:15:00.450
of truth, which could be GCP
IAM, or a Git repository.

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:04.620
The software then automatically
updates all participating

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:08.040
clusters at once, regardless
of where they are.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:13.450
They could be in GCP, or a
different cloud, or on-prem.

00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:18.070
And so it synchronizes
those policies everywhere.

00:15:18.070 --> 00:15:22.410
Our first version supports
synchronizing namespaces,

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:26.710
Role-Based Access Control, or
RBAC policies, quota policies,

00:15:26.710 --> 00:15:28.240
and more.

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:32.410
And we're going to show
you a demo of that now,

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:35.570
actually using the cluster
that Chen provisioned.

00:15:40.180 --> 00:15:44.124
So we can move to
the demo screen.

00:15:44.124 --> 00:15:45.098
There we go.

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:53.560
All right.

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:57.130
So first I want to show you what
clusters I have access to here.

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:58.960
I've got actually four clusters.

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:01.330
I'm going to be using
three of them in this demo.

00:16:01.330 --> 00:16:05.200
I've got US Central, East
Coast, and this puppy

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:08.050
over here, the West cluster.

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:10.570
I've also set up some watches
on these three clusters.

00:16:10.570 --> 00:16:13.750
So here's a watch on East,
here's a watch on Central,

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:16.210
and here's a watch on
the namespaces in West.

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:18.850
And as you can see, there's
really just system namespaces

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:21.100
here today.

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:23.770
As an admin, what I want
to do is, across all

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:27.640
of my environments, I want
to set a set of policies

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:29.380
that apply to all of them.

00:16:29.380 --> 00:16:31.340
And I've created
some policies, first

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:34.660
of all, that I'm going to
copy into this directory.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:36.590
And let me show
you what they are.

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:40.990
So I want each cluster to
have three namespaces--

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:44.500
a dev namespace, a prod
namespace, and a staging

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:46.100
namespace.

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:48.300
And for my SREs, I
want to give them

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:51.570
pod creation access across
all of these three namespaces.

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:56.010
So I've created a pod creator
SRE role binding that,

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.140
in this hierarchical
tree, applies to all three

00:16:58.140 --> 00:16:59.970
of these namespaces.

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:03.120
But in my dev namespace,
that's the only place

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:06.210
that I want to allow
developers to create pods.

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:08.550
I don't want them to
be able to create pods

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:10.530
in production or in staging.

00:17:10.530 --> 00:17:16.530
So I have created this role
and role binding for pod

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:20.099
creation just for the devs,
just in the dev namespace.

00:17:20.099 --> 00:17:23.819
Also, I want to make sure that
my test costs don't balloon out

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:27.780
of control, so I'm going
to set a quota policy which

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:30.750
is encapsulated here
in quota.yaml, also

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:32.610
for the dev space.

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:33.360
All right.

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:34.980
This is cool.

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:36.840
Now what I'm going
to do is add that

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:38.760
to an empty Git repository.

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:40.830
So that's going to be my
single source of truth

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.080
for all of my policies.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:46.570
And then I will go ahead
and commit this file.

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:48.420
And as I push this
change, you will

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:51.360
start to see, in
these three watches,

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:53.790
those three namespaces come up.

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:58.620
So we should see an orders-dev,
orders-prod, and orders-staging

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:00.960
namespace come up in
all three clusters.

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:02.542
Fingers crossed.

00:18:02.542 --> 00:18:06.730
And OK, it looks like US Central
has got orders-dev, prod,

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:07.590
and staging.

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:08.370
Nice.

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:10.620
And US East does as well.

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:14.970
And here is our pet
cluster here on prem

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:18.990
and on the stage, which has GKE
Connect, the agent that Chen

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:20.250
configured earlier.

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:23.190
And there also we have
the three namespaces.

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:26.820
So you see here centralized
policy management synchronized

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:30.000
across multiple environments.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.500
One last thing I'm
going to show you

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:33.490
is that quota that I created.

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:36.630
I want to make sure that
it's also actually enforced

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:38.610
right here in this cluster.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:41.460
So I've changed context
to the West cluster,

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:43.690
and I'm going to see if
there's a quota file.

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:45.420
So let's go ahead and open that.

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:48.240
And indeed, there is
a resource quota file,

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:52.050
and it does set a both
CPU and memory quota limit

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:53.460
for what my devs can do.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:57.190
And so that's it.

00:18:57.190 --> 00:18:59.070
That concludes the demo.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:02.760
In summary, Cloud Services
Platform Policy Management

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:07.320
software allows you to
apply policies across cloud

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:09.240
from one source of truth.

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:11.790
You can kind of think of
it as security and policy

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:12.921
portability.

00:19:16.712 --> 00:19:18.420
CHEN GOLDBERG: That
was an awesome demo--

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:19.950
one of my highlights,
the thing I

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.230
love the most about
this announcement.

00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:24.330
The combination of
consistent experience

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:27.000
and centralized control access
on-prem and in the cloud

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.380
is powerful.

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:31.770
But all of this is ultimately
in the service of your business

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.190
success.

00:19:33.190 --> 00:19:36.280
That depends directly on
developers' productivity

00:19:36.280 --> 00:19:40.100
and on their apps
running reliably.

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:42.650
Often, these two
things are at odds.

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:45.740
Developers want to move
fast, but operation teams

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:47.690
would like to make sure
the environments are

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:51.880
enterprise-ready, and they
would like to have less changes.

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:53.470
That brings us to
the third principle

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:59.320
of Cloud Services Platform,
agility with reliability.

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:03.430
Cloud Services Platform lifts
this trade-off with Istio.

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:06.790
Istio is an open platform to
connect, secure, and manage

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:08.050
services.

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:10.420
It takes care of traffic
management, monitoring,

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:12.310
and security.

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:14.890
It does it by raising
the level of abstractions

00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:20.120
from VMs, containers,
and ports to services.

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.210
This also means
that Istio can work

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:25.180
with uncontainerized
workloads together

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:27.820
with containerized ones.

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:30.460
This week, we are
announcing Istio 1.0,

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:32.670
and its readiness for
production environment.

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:33.545
APARNA SINHA: Woohoo!

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:38.450
CHEN GOLDBERG: This
has two big benefits.

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:40.940
One, it puts more
intelligence in the hands

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:44.780
of the operators, who can now
systematically monitor, secure,

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:46.920
and control services.

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:49.430
The second one, it
relieves the developers

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:52.910
from writing common capabilities
that every app requires,

00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:56.900
like monitoring, authentication,
logging, and more.

00:20:56.900 --> 00:21:01.480
By decoupling development
from operation,

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:05.150
Istio gives coders their time
back, while not compromising

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:07.850
the reliability aspect,
and gives operations

00:21:07.850 --> 00:21:11.930
high-quality common services,
enabling greater visibility,

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:14.390
security, and reliability.

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:16.240
Now we'll go to the
last demo for today.

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:17.240
APARNA SINHA: All right.

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:19.520
So as a developer,
I want to focus

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:22.610
on writing my applications
that actually add value

00:21:22.610 --> 00:21:23.990
to my business.

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:26.150
I don't really want
to write a whole bunch

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:29.150
of common services
for auth or monitoring

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:31.410
or logging or other things.

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:34.070
And those things need
to be done, done well,

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:37.350
in their own right.

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:45.140
So Istio actually enables
us to decouple that work

00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:49.040
from developers, freeing us
to focus on what's important.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:50.570
And I'm going to
demo that for you.

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:53.550
Specifically, this demo is
going to show you three things.

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:57.170
Number one, it's an example
of how Istio frees developers

00:21:57.170 --> 00:22:01.050
from having to implement auth
in each of their services.

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:03.350
Number two, it's
going to show you

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:07.760
how, as a security operator, I
can apply policy incrementally

00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:10.730
to new services, in the
face of existing services,

00:22:10.730 --> 00:22:12.350
without breaking them.

00:22:12.350 --> 00:22:14.290
And then number three,
I'm going to demo

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:18.250
how Istio enables a
secure-by-default enterprise.

00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:20.810
And to do that, I'm going
to be using this application

00:22:20.810 --> 00:22:23.120
called the Bookinfo demo app.

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:25.040
It consists of four services.

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:26.720
As you can see,
these four services

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:29.480
are written in four different
languages, also referred

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:32.030
to as a polyglot application.

00:22:32.030 --> 00:22:34.940
And each of these languages
can have their own way

00:22:34.940 --> 00:22:39.920
of implementing monitoring or
auth or any of these services.

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:44.440
But what we've done here
is we've deployed Istio.

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:50.180
And we've enabled Istio as
an auto-injected on-by-proxy

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:51.320
in each service.

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:54.250
And you kind of see
that with the blue line.

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:58.090
This required zero code changes
from any of the developers.

00:22:58.090 --> 00:23:03.140
And out of the box, it
comes with authentication,

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:05.060
best-in-class authentication.

00:23:05.060 --> 00:23:09.310
It comes with intelligent
routing and telemetry.

00:23:09.310 --> 00:23:12.400
For demo purposes, we
also have two clients.

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:15.380
There's a modern client that's
in the Bookinfo namespace.

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:17.440
So that's going to
be shown in green.

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:19.720
And it's actually going
to get all of the changes

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:22.010
that we make through Istio.

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:26.060
And then there's a red client,
