00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:09.200
And in addition, we've now got
this additional traffic, that's

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:12.050
also one QPS, that is MTLS.

00:27:12.050 --> 00:27:15.770
So this is the MTLS traffic
from the Bookinfo client

00:27:15.770 --> 00:27:18.740
that's in the same namespace.

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:22.820
So you see we've gone down
from two QPS to one QPS, one

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:24.740
QPS each of MTLS and non-MTLS.

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:26.030
So this is permissive mode.

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:26.810
That's great.

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:28.760
And the benefit
here was we didn't

00:27:28.760 --> 00:27:30.590
break the legacy service.

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:33.210
So the legacy service is still
hitting the product page.

00:27:33.210 --> 00:27:34.010
And that's fine.

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:36.050
And SRE can then talk
to the developers

00:27:36.050 --> 00:27:38.450
and say, hey, we got to stop
with this legacy service.

00:27:38.450 --> 00:27:41.390
Please implement client-side
auth in that legacy service,

00:27:41.390 --> 00:27:43.880
because we want everything
to be authenticated,

00:27:43.880 --> 00:27:45.920
because this is a
product page that's

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:48.720
got sensitive information.

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:51.650
So now what we're
going to do is we want

00:27:51.650 --> 00:27:53.180
to turn off the legacy service.

00:27:53.180 --> 00:27:54.890
Turns out it's actually rogue.

00:27:54.890 --> 00:27:58.940
And we want to enable
MTLS-only authentication.

00:27:58.940 --> 00:28:01.850
We only want services that
have client-side authentication

00:28:01.850 --> 00:28:06.630
to communicate with
our product info page.

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:08.780
So this is what the
YAML looks like.

00:28:08.780 --> 00:28:10.700
It's very similar,
so it again applies

00:28:10.700 --> 00:28:11.930
to the Bookinfo namespace.

00:28:11.930 --> 00:28:13.280
But now the mode is strict.

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:15.740
So we're strictly
going to accept traffic

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:19.860
from services, client
services, that understand MTLS.

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:21.890
And so let's go
ahead and apply that.

00:28:21.890 --> 00:28:23.400
And here we go.

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:25.610
And so now that
policy's been applied.

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:28.700
And what we should see now
is only one QPS of traffic,

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:32.210
only from the service that's
in the Bookinfo namespace that

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:33.440
has MTLS enabled.

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:36.260
And the other one, the rogue
client, should go away.

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:38.360
So let's see, did that happen?

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:44.630
And it looks like it did happen.

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:47.170
So you see that the blue
service, the traffic

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:50.920
from the unauthenticated,
non-MTLS service

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:52.750
has gone down to zero QPS.

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:55.240
And now we only just have
the one QPS of traffic

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:57.632
from the MTLS-enabled service.

00:28:57.632 --> 00:28:58.840
And we can see that together.

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:02.200
So actually, if you want, we
can see the history of this.

00:29:02.200 --> 00:29:04.000
Let's just go back 15
minutes, and you'll

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:06.190
see we had both two QPS.

00:29:06.190 --> 00:29:10.820
Then we went to one and one,
and now we just have one.

00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:13.280
So that's nice.

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:15.980
In summary, without
any code changes,

00:29:15.980 --> 00:29:19.760
we have enabled
client-side authentication

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:23.360
across every service in
the Bookinfo namespace.

00:29:23.360 --> 00:29:27.090
And also, by the way, we've
turned on security by default.

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:30.110
So if there are any other
services in the future created

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:36.250
in Bookinfo, they're going
to be MTLS-enabled as well.

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:38.280
Those are not the only
benefits of Istio.

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:41.070
We don't really have time to
demo all of the benefits today.

00:29:41.070 --> 00:29:44.520
So instead, I've
invited a customer

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:46.500
to speak to you
about how they are

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:51.090
using Cloud Services Platform
to solve real-world challenges.

00:29:51.090 --> 00:29:53.850
It's my pleasure,
and please join me

00:29:53.850 --> 00:29:57.750
in welcoming Jeff White,
platform architect at eBay,

00:29:57.750 --> 00:29:59.416
to the stage.

00:29:59.416 --> 00:30:03.898
