applications in Kubernetes.

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:23.450
APARNA SINHA: Wow.

00:32:23.450 --> 00:32:25.970
That sounds like an
amazing charter, Jeff.

00:32:25.970 --> 00:32:30.544
So how's your journey so far?

00:32:30.544 --> 00:32:31.460
JEFF WHITE: Fantastic.

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:36.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:36.610 --> 00:32:38.790
We've learned a
tremendous amount

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:43.380
about building robust
cloud-native services.

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:47.460
Kubernetes and Helm, along
with our internal tools

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:52.690
and standards, have made it
very easy to deploy services.

00:32:52.690 --> 00:32:54.550
And we've scaled
our architecture

00:32:54.550 --> 00:32:57.520
across multiple dimensions.

00:32:57.520 --> 00:33:01.520
We have on the order
of 100 applications,

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:06.200
and they're running a variety
of languages and frameworks,

00:33:06.200 --> 00:33:09.860
and these services are deployed
across multiple clusters

00:33:09.860 --> 00:33:13.695
running around the world.

00:33:13.695 --> 00:33:14.590
APARNA SINHA: Wow.

00:33:14.590 --> 00:33:17.006
JEFF WHITE: And of course,
we've scaled our infrastructure

00:33:17.006 --> 00:33:20.950
horizontally to match our
traffic and workloads.

00:33:23.650 --> 00:33:26.830
But scaling across
these dimensions

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:30.826
presents new
monitoring challenges.

00:33:30.826 --> 00:33:35.800
A microservices architecture
shifts the emphasis

00:33:35.800 --> 00:33:40.200
from monitoring individual
services in isolation

00:33:40.200 --> 00:33:45.950
to monitoring the entire
system as a whole.

00:33:45.950 --> 00:33:50.250
When you get paged, the problem
is never your fault, right?

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:54.240
APARNA SINHA: Right.

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:56.670
JEFF WHITE: It's always,
you can't connect

00:33:56.670 --> 00:34:01.410
to one of your dependencies,
or some error way upstream has

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:03.870
propagated all the way
to you, and now everyone

00:34:03.870 --> 00:34:07.005
is up, trying to figure
out the root cause.

00:34:10.920 --> 00:34:13.949
Standardizing monitoring
signals allows us

00:34:13.949 --> 00:34:18.960
to monitor across the system.

00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:22.310
This allows you to do
things like correlate logs

00:34:22.310 --> 00:34:26.590
by trace ID, and
distributed tracing,

00:34:26.590 --> 00:34:30.690
so you can inspect the
network call stack.

00:34:30.690 --> 00:34:35.810
But these monitoring concepts
require services to collaborate

00:34:35.810 --> 00:34:38.989
on consistent standards,
and they become

00:34:38.989 --> 00:34:42.390
part of the platform itself.

00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:49.989
But adding this logic to our
applications and libraries

00:34:49.989 --> 00:34:52.989
makes it difficult to
migrate, especially

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:55.840
in a polyglot environment.

00:34:55.840 --> 00:34:58.960
Maintaining a services
platform becomes

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:04.997
exponentially difficult as
the architecture scales.

00:35:04.997 --> 00:35:05.830
APARNA SINHA: I see.

00:35:05.830 --> 00:35:07.180
I understand.

00:35:07.180 --> 00:35:10.330
So how are you dealing
with this complexity

00:35:10.330 --> 00:35:15.325
and multi-polyglot
services environment?

00:35:15.325 --> 00:35:17.305
JEFF WHITE: Boom!

00:35:17.305 --> 00:35:21.270
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:24.840
So we looked at service
mesh technology,

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:28.500
and we saw the value of
moving these common networking

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:32.200
concerns from our
application processes

00:35:32.200 --> 00:35:36.560
to an individual,
independent component.

00:35:36.560 --> 00:35:38.510
And we particularly
like how Istio

00:35:38.510 --> 00:35:41.510
makes this transparent
to services,

00:35:41.510 --> 00:35:45.570
with its strong Kubernetes
integration and sidecar proxy

00:35:45.570 --> 00:35:46.070
approach.

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:53.370
So we started experimenting
with Istio earlier this year,

00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:56.240
and it was a natural
decision to initially focus

00:35:56.240 --> 00:35:58.820
on the observability features--

00:35:58.820 --> 00:36:01.295
metrics, traces, and logs.

00:36:04.150 --> 00:36:07.570
These provided us with the
biggest initial benefit

00:36:07.570 --> 00:36:12.590
while minimizing risk
and learning curve.

00:36:12.590 --> 00:36:15.560
And perhaps most
importantly, we would

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:20.740
be able to use the very metrics
provided by Istio to help

00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:25.690
us evaluate and fine-tune as we
incorporate more advanced Istio

00:36:25.690 --> 00:36:28.360
features.

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:31.000
As part of our
testing, we gradually

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:36.680
increased the size of our mesh
by using an opt-in policy.

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:40.911
And today, we are running
Istio in production.

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:45.256
APARNA SINHA: That's awesome.

00:36:45.256 --> 00:36:48.112
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:53.830 --> 00:36:56.560
JEFF WHITE: Our mesh contains
about 10 services so far,

00:36:56.560 --> 00:36:59.800
and we are progressively
adding more.

00:36:59.800 --> 00:37:03.880
And we now have a uniform
and transparent mechanism

00:37:03.880 --> 00:37:07.720
of collecting metrics,
traces, and logs.

00:37:07.720 --> 00:37:11.080
The Prometheus and
Grafana dashboards

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:13.750
provide us with the key metrics.

00:37:13.750 --> 00:37:17.170
We can get an aggregated
view of the overall health

00:37:17.170 --> 00:37:22.460
of the entire service mesh,
showing traffic, latency,

00:37:22.460 --> 00:37:28.370
and errors, three of the four
golden monitoring signals.

00:37:28.370 --> 00:37:31.430
And we can dive into an
individual application

00:37:31.430 --> 00:37:35.820
and see those same very metrics.

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:38.640
And we also have Stackdriver
integration working,

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:42.520
and we're continuing to
explore those capabilities.

00:37:42.520 --> 00:37:45.520
These tools complement
our existing metrics

00:37:45.520 --> 00:37:48.710
and monitoring,
allow us to continue

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:51.980
to refine our
service-level objectives,

00:37:51.980 --> 00:37:57.630
and assist us in troubleshooting
live site issues.

00:37:57.630 --> 00:37:59.340
And we're now in
a great position

00:37:59.340 --> 00:38:05.010
to start introducing some of the
more advanced Istio features.

00:38:05.010 --> 00:38:08.100
We look forward to
seeing how much Istio

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:11.670
simplifies our monitoring
and improves our system

00:38:11.670 --> 00:38:13.599
performance.

00:38:13.599 --> 00:38:14.390
CHEN GOLDBERG: Wow.

00:38:14.390 --> 00:38:15.440
This is awesome to hear.

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:16.310
Thank you, Jeff.

00:38:16.310 --> 00:38:18.470
It was such a great
time to work with you

00:38:18.470 --> 00:38:20.730
and the team over
the past few years--

00:38:20.730 --> 00:38:22.010
few months.

00:38:22.010 --> 00:38:23.600
As we continue
and work on Istio,

00:38:23.600 --> 00:38:26.590
is there any feedback
or request for us,

00:38:26.590 --> 00:38:29.282
